    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21551",
      "problem": "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
